1
00:00:00,578 --> 00:00:04,064
Now, thankfully, this memory problem can be

2
00:00:04,064 --> 00:00:07,550
solved because the goal is not to run these

3
00:00:07,743 --> 00:00:11,310
raw trained models with their float32 or

4
00:00:11,438 --> 00:00:12,900
float16 values.

5
00:00:13,768 --> 00:00:15,471
Because of course, if you were to ask

6
00:00:15,471 --> 00:00:17,543
yourself whether that runs on your machine

7
00:00:17,543 --> 00:00:19,921
or not, the answer would pretty much always

8
00:00:19,953 --> 00:00:22,732
be no, except for super small models.

9
00:00:23,632 --> 00:00:25,383
That's why we have a solution called

10
00:00:25,415 --> 00:00:28,354
quantization. That helps us with running

11
00:00:28,867 --> 00:00:29,637
bigger models

12
00:00:30,455 --> 00:00:32,397
on your or mine machine.

13
00:00:33,215 --> 00:00:35,782
So it's a solution that helps us reduce

14
00:00:35,846 --> 00:00:37,258
these memory requirements.

15
00:00:37,996 --> 00:00:40,579
How does this work? Well, in the end, it's a

16
00:00:40,579 --> 00:00:43,917
compression mechanism. Quantized large

17
00:00:44,013 --> 00:00:46,757
language models are compressed large

18
00:00:46,837 --> 00:00:47,767
language models.

19
00:00:48,602 --> 00:00:50,832
And that's achieved by taking the original

20
00:00:50,848 --> 00:00:53,351
trained model, which is not quantized and

21
00:00:53,351 --> 00:00:54,955
which would therefore take up lots of

22
00:00:55,004 --> 00:00:55,340
memory,

23
00:00:56,366 --> 00:00:59,379
And then through a mathematical process,

24
00:01:00,212 --> 00:01:03,497
these parameter values are transformed to

25
00:01:03,754 --> 00:01:07,936
less precise numbers. So instead of float16,

26
00:01:08,160 --> 00:01:09,218
float32,

27
00:01:09,763 --> 00:01:12,855
they are converted to int4, int8 numbers

28
00:01:12,968 --> 00:01:13,448
typically.

29
00:01:14,249 --> 00:01:15,788
And int numbers are

30
00:01:16,477 --> 00:01:18,496
numbers without a decimal place. So

31
00:01:18,496 --> 00:01:20,467
something like 4, 5, 10,

32
00:01:21,220 --> 00:01:23,916
-3. These would be integer numbers.

33
00:01:24,591 --> 00:01:27,466
And these parameter values are transformed

34
00:01:27,466 --> 00:01:28,735
to such integer numbers

35
00:01:29,409 --> 00:01:31,931
so that they therefore take up way less

36
00:01:32,076 --> 00:01:34,115
space per parameter. And

37
00:01:34,694 --> 00:01:37,569
the great news is that the quantization

38
00:01:37,746 --> 00:01:39,850
algorithms that are used for that

39
00:01:39,850 --> 00:01:40,669
transformation

40
00:01:41,359 --> 00:01:44,427
are so good that the model performance, the

41
00:01:44,492 --> 00:01:46,965
quality of the model is essentially

42
00:01:47,142 --> 00:01:47,897
unchanged.

43
00:01:48,442 --> 00:01:50,975
At least for the commonly used quantization

44
00:01:51,007 --> 00:01:52,498
mechanisms and solutions.

45
00:01:53,331 --> 00:01:55,063
So you get the best of both worlds,

46
00:01:55,640 --> 00:01:58,477
a model that does perform great, but that

47
00:01:58,525 --> 00:02:01,314
does not take up as much space and memory as

48
00:02:01,346 --> 00:02:04,200
the originally trained model would have. In

49
00:02:04,312 --> 00:02:07,037
addition, inference speed may also be

50
00:02:07,037 --> 00:02:07,342
better.

51
00:02:08,480 --> 00:02:10,532
Now, the good news is that you don't need to

52
00:02:10,628 --> 00:02:12,648
perform this quantization yourself.

53
00:02:13,305 --> 00:02:15,982
So in case you were afraid, you don't need

54
00:02:16,094 --> 00:02:18,675
to do that. You don't need to understand the

55
00:02:18,739 --> 00:02:21,177
mathematical process that's being used for

56
00:02:21,289 --> 00:02:22,476
transforming these values.

57
00:02:23,134 --> 00:02:25,075
You just should have heard the term and you

58
00:02:25,075 --> 00:02:26,695
should understand that it's about

59
00:02:26,984 --> 00:02:29,711
compressing these parameter values to make

60
00:02:29,711 --> 00:02:31,877
them way smaller. Because if we're talking

61
00:02:31,909 --> 00:02:36,529
about half a byte or 1 byte instead of 2 or

62
00:02:36,529 --> 00:02:38,839
4 bytes per parameter,

63
00:02:39,416 --> 00:02:41,807
we of course essentially only need around

64
00:02:41,983 --> 00:02:42,673
1/4

65
00:02:43,427 --> 00:02:46,539
of the originally predicted space in memory.

66
00:02:47,228 --> 00:02:50,307
So therefore, this 27 billion parameters

67
00:02:50,403 --> 00:02:52,952
model, all of a sudden, instead of requiring

68
00:02:53,369 --> 00:02:58,003
100 gigabytes of RAM, may only require 25

69
00:02:58,099 --> 00:03:00,745
gigabytes of RAM or even less than that,

70
00:03:01,081 --> 00:03:04,577
maybe only 12 gigabytes of RAM, roughly,

71
00:03:05,250 --> 00:03:07,655
depending on the quantization mechanism

72
00:03:07,719 --> 00:03:08,024
used.

73
00:03:08,553 --> 00:03:10,990
I'll also say right away that the amount of

74
00:03:11,006 --> 00:03:14,549
memory that's required is not just dependent

75
00:03:14,694 --> 00:03:17,900
on the parameters. Though they are the most

76
00:03:17,900 --> 00:03:18,717
important factor,

77
00:03:19,342 --> 00:03:22,260
but it's also some space that's reserved for

78
00:03:22,324 --> 00:03:25,273
the input and output text of that model

79
00:03:25,450 --> 00:03:27,742
because all that text, the so-called

80
00:03:27,854 --> 00:03:30,788
context, also needs to be loaded into memory

81
00:03:30,900 --> 00:03:32,775
in order to be processed by the model.

82
00:03:33,385 --> 00:03:36,094
But unless you're operating with huge

83
00:03:36,094 --> 00:03:39,123
amounts of text, that will not take up as

84
00:03:39,155 --> 00:03:41,768
much space as the model parameters do, for

85
00:03:41,800 --> 00:03:42,361
example.

86
00:03:43,227 --> 00:03:46,804
And that's why typically when we aim to run

87
00:03:47,077 --> 00:03:49,226
open models, no matter if it's locally on

88
00:03:49,258 --> 00:03:51,424
your system or on some rented server,

89
00:03:52,065 --> 00:03:54,520
we don't use the raw models we find on

90
00:03:54,696 --> 00:03:57,246
Hugging Face. That's also why if I were to

91
00:03:57,278 --> 00:03:58,834
click use this model here,

92
00:03:59,524 --> 00:04:02,860
I don't see any local options because this

93
00:04:03,197 --> 00:04:05,427
is simply not prepared to be executed

94
00:04:05,443 --> 00:04:05,828
locally.

95
00:04:06,903 --> 00:04:08,715
Instead on Hugging Face for all these

